1. We have disabled node-to-node communication in the trace
2. We try to remove DRAM variance by setting it to a singular latency penalty
3. CXL mappings are functioning in both StarNUMA and SST
4. Rejected for load40 mem60->mem100 latency drop: end-of-run censoring / no final drain. It exists in logic, but mem60 has more uncompleted demand requests than mem100, so it cannot explain mem100 reporting lower miss latency.
5. Rejected for load40 mem60->mem100 latency drop: pool response burst / sender-side burst artifact. The pool controller, pool response sender, and switch pool-port all show burst size ~1 packet, so the drop is not coming from multi-packet response dumps.
6. Rejected for load40 mem60->mem100 latency drop: switch node-facing egress blockage on responses. Switch node-port response egress wait stays at 1 cycle with zero blocked cycles in both runs, so the switch is not holding responses because it cannot inject to the node.
7. Rejected for load40 mem60->mem100 latency drop: pool memory controller becoming dramatically faster. Pool demand total service drops only about 1 cycle between the two runs, far too small to explain the ~20 cycle miss-latency drop.
8. Rejected as the root cause of load40 mem60->mem100 response-queue drop: late LLC timer start. It can bias top-line avg_miss_lat/avg_cxl_miss_lat, but it cannot explain why stat.switch.port.pool.0.ingress_queue_wait_avg_cycles.response itself falls, because that stat is measured directly in the switch port's FabricPort ingress release path.
9. Rejected for load40 mem60->mem100 switch response-queue drop: end-of-run censoring of unreleased switch-response packets. The switch pool ingress queue holds at most 1024 B = 16 response packets, so even worst-case omitted packets cannot move the reported average by more than a tiny fraction of a cycle.
10. Rejected for load40 mem60->mem100 switch response-queue drop: queueing moved from switch ingress into switch ready/egress stages. Switch pool ready_wait stays 0 with ready_occ_max 1, and switch node-facing response egress stays unblocked, so the missing delay is not hiding in later switch stages.
11. Rejected for load40 mem60->mem100 switch response-queue drop: response-class/service-floor accounting bug. Pool responses are all emitted as 64 B packets, response classification is pure by payload shape, and the switch response queue-wait max stays the same 315 cycles in both runs, so the average drop is not coming from a changed floor or mixed packet sizes.
12. Rejected for load40 mem60->mem100 latency drop: pool mem_queue_size / link-credit-window cap being the active throttle. In the current single-node runs the implied pool mem queue cap is 128 requests, while observed pool mem queue max occupancy stays around 17, so that bound is not active.
13. Rejected for load40 mem60->mem100 latency drop: response composition shifting between LOAD and RFO. The completed response mix stays close to the configured 40/60 split in both runs, and both per-type response-ready gaps shrink at mem100, so the queue drop is not caused by one class disappearing.
14. Rejected for load40 mem60->mem100 latency drop: shared RNG stream for do_mem/do_load/is_cxl as a sufficient root cause by itself. Even though the runs diverge, that does not by itself explain why the higher-mem run specifically gets fewer sub-25-cycle response arrivals at the switch.
15. Rejected for load40 mem60->mem100 latency drop: node remote-port demand-request egress blocking as the hidden smoother. Node demand-request egress blocked cycles stay 0 and egress occupancy stays 0 in both runs, so node.request_issue is not being spaced out by local fabric-port backpressure.
16. Rejected for load40 mem60->mem100 latency drop: local DRAM/TLB/translation pressure. Local dram_avg stays near zero in both runs, and translation-class pool traffic is negligible, so the mem60->mem100 shift is not being created by local translation bottlenecks.
17. Rejected as a root cause of load40 mem60->mem100 latency drop: warm-cache contamination of llc.remote_* timeline rows. The contamination is real and must be filtered out, but it only invalidates those LLC-stage diagnostics; it does not create the drop in node.request_issue or the switch response queue.
